  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday) \nIssued at 1034 PM CDT Sat Jun 20 2026\n\nBy early Monday, the cold front is expected to be positioned over \nthe southern TX Panhandle with a relatively broad surface trough to \nits south over much of West TX. Model consensus currently favors a \nrelatively slow southward progression of the front on Monday, with \nsoutherly flow associated with the above-mentioned surface troughing \nlikely to result in triple digit temperatures once again over our\nsouthern zones Monday afternoon. Although surface convergence will\nintensify near the front and within the surface trough, thunderstorm\ndevelopment Monday afternoon and evening is still uncertain given\nrelatively weak upper level forcing and what will likely be a \nfairly strong capping inversion. Some models highlight a weak \nshortwave disturbance approaching from the WNW by Monday afternoon\nwhich could support development of some isolated storms Monday \nevening along the front in the Rolling Plains. Given forecast \nstrong to extreme instability and bulk shear magnitudes over 40 \nkt, a conditional severe threat will exist with any thunderstorms \nthat are able to develop Monday evening. \n\nFor the rest of the week, models are in good agreement that the \noverall synoptic pattern will remain rather consistent with a ~597dam\nH5 ridge centered somewhere near El Paso for several days. A train\nof shortwave disturbances within a belt of relatively strong WNW \nflow aloft will bring at least low chances for thunderstorms \nmoving from the CO/NM high terrain into our area each evening from\nTuesday through at least the end of the week, but confidence in \nthis is low with the potential very sensitive to the precise \npositioning of the ridge. Regardless of precipitation chances, \nthis setup will favor temperatures near or above average for this \ntime of year for the middle and end of the week.",
